: "2026-04-28T18:17:33Z", "username": "Sidhesh K K", "text": "Q6. As Crusaders sailed east in search of holy relics, this merchant’s son traveled among Muslim ports of North Africa, studying mathematics preserved by Islamic scholars. He returned to Europe with Hindu-Arabic numerals, including zero—initially distrusted but ultimately transformative. His work reshaped commerce, architecture, and science, achieving far more than any Crusader conquest.\nID X , who symbolized the intellectual exchange overshadowed by the Crusades", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-28T18:17:44Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "leonard", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-28T18:17:45Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "of pisa", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-28T18:17:46Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Fibonnqcu", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-28T18:17:48Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "fibonacci", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-28T18:17:50Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Fibonacci", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-28T18:17:50Z", "username": "AS", "text": "Fibo", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-28T18:18:04Z", "username": "Sidhesh K K", "text": "Fibonacci is correct", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-28T18:18:37Z", "username": "Sidhesh K K", "text": "Apratim 5, Pavan 35, Srijan 15, KP 5", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-28T18:19:08Z", "username": "Sidhesh K K", "text": "Q7. Known as wolfsbane, this poison haunted Crusader-era fear and folklore. Rumored to taint arrows and wells, it was often blamed for sudden deaths during sieges, even when disease was the real cause. Medieval physicians knew it had medicinal uses in tiny doses, but its lethality made it a weapon of paranoia, later embedded into European witchcraft legends.\nWhich poisonous plant embodied the unseen dangers of Crusader warfare", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}]